From 2774f67208b91505659c3c2986a48003279fc583 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 05:41:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Fix dead controls on real phones; give Snake four arrow
 keys
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Pong and Neon Nebula were unplayable on a phone. Both were driven only by
handlers built on Pointer Events, and mobile browsers cancel the pointer
stream out from under you the moment they suspect the gesture might be a
scroll or an edge swipe. The game loop kept running, so it looked frozen
rather than broken. Desktop Chromium never reproduces it, which is why it
shipped and why the earlier tests passed.

The tell was which games survived: Snake, Tetris and Flappy drove their
buttons with onclick and worked, while every control routed through
bindHold or the canvas drag was dead. Pong and Nebula had nothing else.

- bindCanvasDrag and bindHold are now built on Touch Events with a
  non-passive preventDefault, which holds a gesture reliably on mobile.
  Mouse events are bound alongside them, with move/up on window so a drag
  survives leaving the element - what pointer capture used to buy us.
  preventDefault on touchstart also suppresses the synthesised mouse
  events, so one tap can't fire both paths.
- Buttons are back on every game that has them, alongside the gestures.
  Pong keeps UP/DOWN, Nebula and Arena keep their D-pad, and Arena's
  spare slot now carries the ability that used to be E-only. No game can
  be dead if a browser refuses a gesture.
- Pressing a direction clears any stale drag claim, so a gesture the
  browser cut off mid-flight can't leave the buttons inert.
- A tap on Pong's UP/DOWN and Nebula's arrows now nudges immediately as
  well as starting the glide. Hold-to-move alone meant a quick tap moved
  the paddle two pixels and read as a dead button.

Snake gets four absolute arrows (← ↑ ↓ →) in place of the two relative
turns plus a cycle button, which had you working out "which way is left
of me right now" while the snake was already moving. Swipes still work
and map to the same four directions.

Also: the purple accent is now opt-in rather than attached to the fourth
slot, so Snake's ↓ doesn't stand out as if it were special, and bare
glyph buttons get their own type size instead of the label size.

Verified by driving each game through the real path - tap the card, sit
through the countdown - and sampling the canvas to confirm the specific
object moved: Pong's paddle tracks a drag and its button, Nebula's ship
tracks a drag while hold-to-fire puts bullets on screen, and each of
Snake's four arrows steers the right way.

